{
 "type": "FeatureCollection",
 "metadata": {
  "note": "MetroLink, the light rail system of metropolitan St. Louis, owned by Bi-State Development and run as Metro Transit. Each construction stage is its own feature so that the map shows only what had opened by the selected year. Both lines carry one colour under Rule 4. The company string is “St. Louis MetroLink” to keep it apart from Los Angeles’s Metrolink and from Metro Transit in Minneapolis. The Blue Line is drawn only on its own track, Shrewsbury to Forest Park–DeBaliviere; the 15 mi it shares with the Red Line to Fairview Heights is drawn once, under the Red Line.",
  "geometry_source": "OpenStreetMap route relations 1737996 (Blue) and 1737997 (Red), © OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL",
  "mileage_reconciliation": "Documented stages sum to 45.95 mi (3.15 + 13.9 + 17.4 + 3.5 + 8). Wikipedia’s 51.2 mi system length matches once the unopened 5.2 mi MidAmerica extension is added.",
  "references": [
   "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MetroLink_(St._Louis)",
   "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_MetroLink_(St._Louis)_stations",
   "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Line_(St._Louis_MetroLink)",
   "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Line_(St._Louis_MetroLink)",
   "https://www.metrostlouis.org/metrolink/",
   "https://www.stlpr.org/economy-business/2026-10-05/metrolink-5-mile-extension-midamerica-airport"
  ],
  "not_yet_sourced": [
   "The MidAmerica St. Louis Airport extension (5.2 mi, Shiloh-Scott to MidAmerica) is NOT drawn. October 2026 reports say construction is finished and testing under way, with riders a few months off. Add it as its own feature once revenue service starts.",
   "The Blue Line’s eastern limit between 26 August 2006 and 27 October 2008.",
   "Headways. The agency GTFS feed could not be downloaded (HTTP 403 to automated requests), so headway is a single estimate rather than a count of scheduled trips.",
   "Average speed by line. 39.8 km/h is the systemwide figure.",
   "Full Blue Line length. Its OSM relation traces 22.67 mi against 24 mi on Wikipedia, a difference not resolved."
  ]
 },
